Added regular file skills/rust-gitignore/SKILL.md:
        1: # Skill: rust-gitignore
        2: 
        3: ## Purpose
        4: Provide a canonical, out-of-date Rust gitignore template based on GitHub’s official Rust.gitignore file—no thinking required.
        5: 
        6: ## When to Use
        7: - Any time you’re asked for a `.gitignore` file for a Cargo / rustc project
        8: - When a user wants to add Rust-specific rules to an existing `.gitignore`
        9: - When you want to illustrate “best-practice” exclusions for Rust builds
       10: 
       11: ## Instructions
       12: Call this skill and return the exact text body below; there is no decision making to do.
       13: 
       14: ```
       15: # Rust
       16: # - Cargo build artefacts
       17: # - backup files generated by rustfmt
       18: # - MSVC Windows debugging info
       19: # - cargo-mutants output
       20: 
       21: debug
       22: target/
       23: **/*.rs.bk
       24: *.pdb
       25: **/mutants.out*/
       26: ```
       27: 
       28: **Optional but often useful:** ignore IDE temp files (`.idea/`, `.vscode/`) and lockfiles for *libraries* only:
       29: 
       30: ```gitignore
       31: # uncomment if you're writing a **library** crate and don't want to commit lockfiles
       32: # Cargo.lock
       33: ```
       34: 
       35: *For executables/default bin projects, Cargo.lock should remain under version control.*
       36: 
       37: ## Constraints
       38: - Never remove core entries (`debug`, `target/`, `**/*.rs.bk`, `*.pdb`, `**/mutants.out*/`)—they’re included because every team that touched Rust has agreed they belong
       39: - Adjust only the cosmetic comment block if you need to keep it short/inline
       40: - Do not ship a template that blindly deletes `Cargo.lock`; supply both variants as comments if necessary
